"# Registering and calling tools\n\nUse aos-tools to edit a registry and call a registered tool with JSON on stdin.\n\n```sh\naos-tools --file tools.json add --name read ../lib/base/read\naos-tools --file tools.json ls --json\nprintf '%s' '{\"path\":\"a.txt\"}' | aos-tools --file tools.json call read\naos-tools --file tools.json rm read\n```\n\nThe default registry is `./tools.json`; a missing registry means no tools. `--file` goes before or after the subcommand. A registry may carry `\"$override\": OTHER_FILE` (or a list of files): it then holds only the differences from that file, later wins, and an entry whose value is `null` is not a tool. add/rm write only the `--file` registry, never the file it overrides. `aos-tools --file F override BASE` turns F into such a list of differences.\n\nadd accepts a folder, JSON file or executable. Paths resolve from cwd. The stored path keeps the form you gave: an absolute path is stored absolute; a relative path is stored relative to the folder of the registry written. `--path-json JSON` stores a path string or directive as given, e.g. `{\"$env\": \"AOS_HOME\", \"$path\": \"/lib/base/shell\"}` (set `AOS_HOME` once per machine); it is resolved on every use and never written back. A path that resolves to nothing gives `PathMissing` naming the file and field it came from. It prints one line `added NAME -> PATH`. `--name` overrides the default file/folder name. A duplicate gives `ToolExists`; `--replace` replaces it. Flags may go before or after the path. `--no-cache` is for executable tool definitions only.\n\nrm matches a registered name first, otherwise a path. It removes all matches and prints `removed NAME` for each; files remain. ls prints NAME TAB the first sentence of its description (short; see one in full with get); `ls -l` prints NAME TAB KIND TAB PATH TAB FROM TAB STORED TAB ENV; `--json` prints an array with tool definitions. get prints matching definitions as JSON objects. Broken registered tools are errors.\n\nEvery tool gets its JSON input unchanged on stdin. `aos-tools call` gives it a scratch stack: `<parent>/tools/<registered-name>/` when `$AOS_STACK` is set, otherwise aos-exec's default. A registry `env` cannot change that stack.\n\ncall returns the tool's exit code (signal N = 128+N). A nonzero exit with `aos-tools: error:` means aos-tools failed; without that line, it is the tool's code.\n"
